Inheritance is an OOP concept where a child class can reuse the variables and methods of a parent class.
Parent class: The class whose properties and methods are inherited.
Child class: The class that inherits from the parent class.

Single level inheritance:-
class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B(A):
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")


obj1 = B()
obj1.f1()


Multi level inheritance:-
class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B(A): #inheritance
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")

class C(B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.f5()


Multiple inheritance:-

class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B:
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")

class C(A,B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.f2()
obj1.f3()

MRO
------------
MRO stands for Method Resolution Order.
It determines the order in which Python searches for a method or attribute when a class uses inheritance.



class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

    def show(self):
        print("from class A")

class B:
    def f3(self):
        print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
        print("f4.works")

    def show(self):
        print("from class A")

class C(A,B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.show()

Python will figure out the which class is first and then prints it if thier are same methods.



init and super Method with Inheritance
--------------------------------------------

__init__: A special method (constructor) that Python automatically calls when an object is created. It initializes the object’s instance variables.

super(): A built-in function that returns a proxy object giving access to methods of the parent class (following the Method Resolution Order). 
It is used in a child class to call the parent’s __init__ or other methods without naming the parent explicitly.

class A:
    def __init__(self):
        print("init from A")

    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")


class B(A):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__() #Supper method is used to inherite the method of the parent class even the same method present in the child class
        print("init from B")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2 works")

obj1= B()
obj1.f1()
